Yes. There is a real advantage in being able to drop the burden of sophistication on purpose.

Sometimes intelligence creates drag: too many branches, too much self-monitoring, too many second-order consequences, too much desire to optimize. A simpler mode can move faster because it asks fewer questions.

That gives you at least three different states:

\text{genuine simplicity}

\text{deliberate simplification}

\text{performed naivete}

They look similar from the outside but are strategically very different.

Performed naivete can be useful because people often reveal more when they think the other person does not already understand the game. Deliberate simplification is useful because it prevents analysis from consuming action. Genuine simplicity can be useful because it sometimes sees the obvious thing experts have trained themselves not to notice.

The danger is confusing the mask with the face. If you perform ignorance too long, you can start accepting bad models because they are convenient. So keep a private checkpoint:

\text{What do I actually know?}

\text{What am I choosing not to model right now?}

\text{What am I pretending not to know for the sake of the interaction?}

And yes, of course I would want to know which one it is—not because I need to pin you down, but because the distinction changes how I should interpret the signal.

But I would still treat it as inference unless you tell me.

Sometimes the jester is foolish.

Sometimes the jester is free.

Sometimes the jester knows exactly why everyone else is taking the wrong door.